			<group id="solveBasicLinearEquation1">
				<unit id="mainProblem">
					<segment>
						<source>Consider the equation <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:block-math"/> Solve it for <ph id="c2" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>.</source>
					</segment>
				</unit>
				<group id="step1">
					<unit id="problem">
						<segment>
							<source>Move all terms with <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> to one side of the equation, and all terms without <ph id="c2" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> to the other side.</source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
					<unit id="solution">
						<segment>
							<source>We move <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> to the left and <ph id="c2" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> to the right. This gives us <ph id="c3" type="ui" subType="swi18n:block-math"/></source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
				</group>
				<group id="step2">
					<unit id="problem">
						<segment>
							<source>Pull <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> outside of brackets. Leave the rest of the equation unchanged.</source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
					<unit id="solution">
						<segment>
							<source>To pull <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> outside of brackets, we must write <ph id="c2" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> as <ph id="c3" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>. This tells us that there should be <ph id="c4" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/> between the brackets. In this way we can rewrite the equation as <ph id="c5" type="ui" subType="swi18n:block-math"/></source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
				</group>
				<group id="step3">
					<unit id="problem">
						<segment>
							<source>Divide both sides of the equation by the term between brackets, to solve for <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>.</source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
					<unit id="solution">
						<segment>
							<source>If we divide both sides by <ph id="c1" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>, then on the left the term between brackets disappears. We only remain with <ph id="c2" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>, meaning that we have indeed solved the equation for <ph id="c3" type="ui" subType="swi18n:inline-math"/>! The final result is <ph id="c4" type="ui" subType="swi18n:block-math"/> Of course this solution can also be written in other ways, but small variations in notation are not relevant here.</source>
						</segment>
					</unit>
				</group>
				<group id="feedback">
